Create and Attach EBS Volume + Increase CPU/RAM
PART A — Create and Attach EBS Volume
STEP 1 — Login to AWS
Open browser
Go to AWS Console
Login with your AWS account

After login, you will see:

Search bar on top
Services
EC2 option
STEP 2 — Open EC2 Dashboard
Where is the button?

Top search bar → type:

EC2

Click:

EC2

Now EC2 Dashboard opens.

STEP 3 — Open Volumes Section

Left side menu → scroll little down

You will see:

Elastic Block Store

Under it click:

Volumes

(Exactly as shown in page 1 images)

STEP 4 — Create Volume

Top right corner click:

Create Volume

Now new page opens.

STEP 5 — Fill Volume Details

You will see options:

Volume Type

Choose:

gp3
Size

Enter:

10 GB

(or 20 GB)

Availability Zone

IMPORTANT:

Choose SAME zone as EC2 instance.

Example:

us-east-1a

If wrong zone → volume cannot attach.

STEP 6 — Create Volume

Scroll down

Click orange button:

Create Volume

Done ✅

STEP 7 — Attach Volume to EC2

Go back to:

Volumes

Click newly created volume checkbox.

Top side click:

Actions

Dropdown opens.

Click:

Attach Volume

(Shown clearly in page 4 screenshot)

STEP 8 — Select Instance

Now screen asks:

Instance

Choose your EC2 instance.

Device Name

Type:

/dev/xvdf

Now click:

Attach Volume

Done ✅

STEP 9 — Connect to EC2 Instance

Go left menu:

Instances

Select instance.

Top click:

Connect

Then click:

EC2 Instance Connect

Then click orange button:

Connect

Terminal opens.

STEP 10 — Check Attached Disk

Inside terminal type:

lsblk

Press Enter.

You will see new disk like:

nvme1n1

(Shown in page 5 and 13)

PART B — Increase CPU and RAM

This means changing instance type.

Example:

t2.micro → low RAM
t2.medium → more RAM + CPU
STEP 1 — Go to Instances

Left menu:

Instances

Select your instance.

STEP 2 — Stop Instance

Top click:

Instance State

Click:

Stop Instance

Wait until state becomes:

Stopped
STEP 3 — Change Instance Type

Top click:

Actions

Then:

Instance Settings

Then:

Change Instance Type

(Shown in page 9 image)

STEP 4 — Select Bigger Instance

Choose example:

t2.small

or

t2.medium

These give more:

CPU
RAM

Now click:

Apply
STEP 5 — Start Instance Again

Top click:

Instance State

Click:

Start Instance

Done ✅

Your EC2 now has more CPU and RAM.

EXPERIMENT 2
Permanently Mount EBS Volume

Goal:
Even after reboot, data should remain.

STEP 1 — Connect to EC2

Open terminal using Connect button.

STEP 2 — Check Disks

Type:

lsblk

You will see:

nvme0n1 → old disk
nvme1n1 → new disk
STEP 3 — Create Partition

Type:

sudo fdisk /dev/nvme1n1

Now terminal asks commands.

Type one by one:

n

Press Enter.

Then:

p

Press Enter.

Press Enter 3 more times.

Then type:

w

Press Enter.

Done partition created ✅

(Page 14 shows this clearly)

STEP 4 — Inform Linux

Type:

sudo partprobe
STEP 5 — Verify Partition

Type:

lsblk

Now you should see:

nvme1n1p1
STEP 6 — Format Partition

Type:

sudo mkfs.xfs /dev/nvme1n1p1

This prepares disk for storage.

STEP 7 — Create Folder for Mounting

Type:

sudo mkdir /mnt/archana

You can use any name.

Example:

/mnt/data
STEP 8 — Mount Volume

Type:

sudo mount /dev/nvme1n1p1 /mnt/archana
STEP 9 — Check Mounted Storage

Type:

df -h

You will see mounted folder.

(Page 17 shows this output)

STEP 10 — Permanent Mount Using fstab
Get UUID

Type:

sudo blkid /dev/nvme1n1p1

Copy UUID value.

Example:

1234-abcd
Open fstab file

Type:

sudo nano /etc/fstab
Add This Line at Bottom
UUID=your-uuid /mnt/archana xfs defaults,nofail 0 0

Example:

UUID=1234-abcd /mnt/archana xfs defaults,nofail 0 0
Save File

Press:

CTRL + O

Press Enter.

Then:

CTRL + X

Done ✅

STEP 11 — Test

Type:

sudo mount -a

No error means success.

STEP 12 — Reboot

Type:

sudo reboot

Reconnect again.

Type:

df -h

If mount still exists → permanent mounting successful ✅

EXPERIMENT 3
Snapshot + Copy to Another Region
STEP 1 — Create Snapshot

Go to:

EC2 → Volumes

Select volume.

Top click:

Actions

Click:

Create Snapshot

(Page 22-23 images)

STEP 2 — Enter Snapshot Details

Give:

Name
Description

Click orange button:

Create Snapshot
STEP 3 — Copy Snapshot to Another Region

Go left menu:

Snapshots

Select snapshot.

Top click:

Actions

Click:

Copy Snapshot

(Page 24-25 shows exact buttons)

STEP 4 — Choose Destination Region

Top dropdown asks region.

Example:

Mumbai

or

Ohio

Click:

Copy Snapshot

Wait until status becomes:

Completed
STEP 5 — Switch Region

Top right corner AWS region dropdown.

Change to destination region.

Example:

N. Virginia → Mumbai
STEP 6 — Create Volume from Snapshot

Go:

Snapshots

Select copied snapshot.

Top click:

Actions

Then:

Create Volume
STEP 7 — Attach to New EC2

Go:

Volumes

Select new volume.

Click:

Actions → Attach Volume

Choose instance.

Click:

Attach

Done ✅
